WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.223 --> 00:00:03.373
The third case is infinity minus infinity.

2
00:00:03.423 --> 00:00:06.305
It need not be zero. Take the square root

3
00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:07.918
of x squared plus x,

4
00:00:07.969 --> 00:00:11.145
minus x, as x tends to positive infinity.

5
00:00:11.335 --> 00:00:13.417
To remove this difference involving a root,

6
00:00:13.511 --> 00:00:17.308
multiply and divide by the conjugate. Here it is

7
00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:18.936
the same square root plus x.

8
00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:22.088
Use the difference of two squares in the numerator.

9
00:00:22.234 --> 00:00:24.444
It becomes x squared plus x,

10
00:00:24.495 --> 00:00:27.075
minus x squared. Only x remains.

11
00:00:27.268 --> 00:00:29.138
Since x tends to positive infinity,

12
00:00:29.146 --> 00:00:30.413
it is eventually positive.

13
00:00:30.586 --> 00:00:32.690
Divide numerator and denominator by x.

14
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.024
We get one over the square root of one plus

15
00:00:35.024 --> 00:00:36.907
one over x, with one added outside the root.

16
00:00:37.072 --> 00:00:39.079
One over x tends to zero.

17
00:00:39.226 --> 00:00:41.905
The result is one divided by one plus one,

18
00:00:41.913 --> 00:00:45.625
which is one half. Infinity minus infinity

19
00:00:45.625 --> 00:00:47.149
has given one half here, rather than zero.
